---
/**
 * Layer 2: the Birth Time Toolkit itself.
 *
 * This page is both the web-readable version and the source the PDF is
 * rendered from, so the download cannot drift from what is on the site. One
 * document, two presentations.
 *
 * `noindex` on purpose. /birth-time/ is the page built to rank for this topic;
 * this one would compete with it for the same queries while being a worse
 * search result — a worksheet set is for someone who has already decided to do
 * the work. It stays reachable and linkable for anyone who prefers reading it
 * in a browser, or who cannot use a PDF.
 *
 * The worksheets print. `.ws-line` and `.ws-grid` collapse to ruled writing
 * space under the PDF generator's stylesheet, which is why they are markup
 * rather than a picture of a form: it keeps the whole thing accessible,
 * translatable and editable, and means the PDF is generated rather than
 * maintained.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../../config/site';

const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Birth Time Toolkit', href: '/birth-time-toolkit/' },
  { label: 'The worksheets', href: '/birth-time-toolkit/worksheets/' },
];

/** A ruled writing line with a label. Repeats enough to be worth a helper. */
const lines = (n: number) => Array.from({ length: n });
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Birth Time Toolkit: worksheets"
  description="The full Birth Time Toolkit: a record search checklist, family interview sheets, a competing-times comparison, a confidence scale, and a reading preparation summary."
  path="/birth-time-toolkit/worksheets/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  noindex={true}
>
  <section class="section toolkit">
    <div class="container prose flow" style="max-width: 44rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Mo Lumen Astrology</p>
      <h1>The Birth Time Toolkit</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A working process for finding a birth time, and for staying clear about what you know when
        the exact time remains uncertain. Print it, or work through it on screen and keep your notes
        wherever you keep notes.
      </p>
      <p>
        The background, why the time matters, how much precision actually matters, and what a
        reading can still do without it, is on
        <a href="/birth-time/">how to find your birth time</a>. This is the part where you go and
        do it.
      </p>

      <hr class="rule-print" />

      {/*
        The `id` on every section heading is not decoration. The Birth Time
        Confidence check on /birth-time-toolkit/ sends each result straight to
        the worksheet that matches it, and a result whose link lands at the top
        of a thirteen-page document has not sent anyone anywhere. The slugs are
        descriptive rather than numbered so that renumbering a section does not
        break an inbound link, and `content-integrity.mjs` asserts that every
        anchor the tool emits exists here.
      */}
      <h2 id="what-you-know">1 · What do you already know?</h2>
      <p>
        Start here even if the answer is "almost nothing". Writing down what you have, and where it
        came from, is what turns a vague uncertainty into a search with a shape.
      </p>
      <div class="ws">
        <div class="ws-grid">
          <div><span class="ws-label">Birth date</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">Birth city</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">State / province</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">Country</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">Hospital or place</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">Time you have been told</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">Who told you</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">How sure are they</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="ws-note">
          If the time came from a document, note which document. If it came from a person, note the
          person. Those are different kinds of answer and they get weighed differently later.
        </p>
      </div>

      <hr class="rule-print" />

      <h2 id="record-search">2 · Record search checklist</h2>
      <p>
        Start with the sources most likely to have been recorded close to the birth, then widen the
        search if you need to. Availability, retention, fees, and whether a time appears at all vary
        by place and by record.
      </p>
      <div class="ws">
        <ul class="ws-check">
          <li>
            <strong>Check which birth certificate you have.</strong> Some abbreviated copies omit
            information that appears on a fuller record. Look for a long-form or full version if
            your jurisdiction offers one.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Request the full certificate</strong> from the vital records authority for the
            place of birth. Search for that office by name and read its own instructions;
            requirements, fees, and whether the time is included vary by jurisdiction and record.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Try the hospital's medical records department.</strong> It is separate from the
            civil registry and has its own release and retention rules.
          </li>
          <li><strong>Baby book, hospital keepsake, or bracelet card.</strong></li>
          <li>
            <strong>Family papers</strong>: a parent's diary, a family Bible, a letter written that
            week, or the back of a photograph.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Religious or community records</strong> where a birth or naming was registered
            locally.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Newspaper birth announcements.</strong> Library and newspaper archives may be
            worth checking, especially when other records are unavailable.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <div class="ws-grid ws-grid--wide">
          <div><span class="ws-label">Office / source contacted</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">Date contacted</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">What they said</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">Follow up by</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">Office / source contacted</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">Date contacted</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">What they said</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">Follow up by</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <hr class="rule-print" />

      <h2 id="family-interview">3 · Family interview</h2>
      <p>
        Ask about the shape of the day as well as the number on the clock. A remembered event can
        give useful context to an approximate time and helps you keep a recollection from becoming
        more precise on paper than it was in the person's memory.
      </p>
      <p>Prompts worth using:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Was it light or dark outside?</li>
        <li>Before or after a meal, which one?</li>
        <li>Had anyone left for work, or come home?</li>
        <li>Did anyone miss something because of it?</li>
        <li>Who else was there, and had they already arrived?</li>
        <li>Was it the same day labour started?</li>
        <li>Did the hospital staff change shift while you were there?</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="ws">
        {
          lines(2).map(() => (
            <div class="ws-block">
              <div class="ws-grid">
                <div><span class="ws-label">Person</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
                <div><span class="ws-label">Relationship</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
                <div><span class="ws-label">Date asked</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
                <div><span class="ws-label">Time they give</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
              </div>
              <span class="ws-label">What they remember, in their words</span>
              <span class="ws-line ws-line--tall"></span>
              <span class="ws-line ws-line--tall"></span>
              <div class="ws-grid">
                <div><span class="ws-label">Anchored to what event</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
                <div><span class="ws-label">Confidence (see §5)</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <hr class="rule-print" />

      <h2 id="when-times-disagree">4 · When times disagree</h2>
      <p>
        Keep every candidate. A recorded conflict is more useful than choosing one too early,
        because the disagreement itself tells you what still needs checking.
      </p>
      <p>
        A contemporaneous record will usually carry more weight than a later recollection, but keep
        any conflict visible rather than treating one source as infallible. If two candidate times
        produce the same rising sign, one important uncertainty may be reduced, but the exact
        ascendant, angles, and house placements can still change with time and house system.
      </p>
      <div class="ws">
        <table class="ws-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Candidate time</th>
              <th scope="col">Source</th>
              <th scope="col">Document or memory?</th>
              <th scope="col">Confidence</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              lines(4).map(() => (
                <tr>
                  <td></td>
                  <td></td>
                  <td></td>
                  <td></td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <span class="ws-label">What contradicts what, and anything still to check</span>
        <span class="ws-line ws-line--tall"></span>
        <span class="ws-line ws-line--tall"></span>
      </div>

      <hr class="rule-print" />

      <h2 id="confidence-scale">5 · How confident are you?</h2>
      <p>
        Use the plainest label that is true. The point of this scale is to stop a guess quietly
        becoming a fact somewhere between your notes and your astrologer, so resist rounding
        upward.
      </p>
      <dl class="ws-scale">
        <dt>Documented</dt>
        <dd>Written on a birth certificate, hospital record, or contemporaneous document you have seen.</dd>
        <dt>Strongly remembered</dt>
        <dd>One consistent account from someone who was there, anchored to an event as well as a time.</dd>
        <dt>Approximate</dt>
        <dd>A window rather than an exact time: "sometime in the morning" or "between two and four".</dd>
        <dt>Conflicting</dt>
        <dd>More than one candidate, none clearly better. Keep and share them all.</dd>
        <dt>Unknown</dt>
        <dd>No usable time information. Say so plainly so the chart can be handled accordingly.</dd>
      </dl>
      <div class="ws">
        <div class="ws-grid">
          <div><span class="ws-label">My current best time</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">My confidence label</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <hr class="rule-print" />

      <h2 id="if-it-stays-unknown">6 · If it stays unknown</h2>
      <p>
        An unknown birth time changes what can be interpreted confidently; it does not erase the
        chart. The rising sign, exact ascendant and other angles, and houses cannot be established
        reliably from an unknown time. The Moon also moves quickly enough that its degree is
        uncertain across the day and, on some dates, its sign can change.
      </p>
      <p>
        A substantial amount of information may still be usable, including planetary signs and many
        aspects when the positions are stable across the possible time range. Timing work can also
        use contacts to reliably placed natal planets, while avoiding claims that depend on houses,
        angles, or another time-sensitive factor. The exact limits depend on the chart, the date,
        and the technique being used.
      </p>
      <p>
        The important thing is to label a guess as a guess. Apparent precision can make an
        interpretation sound firmer than the underlying birth data supports.
      </p>

      <hr class="rule-print" />

      <h2 id="rectification">7 · About rectification</h2>
      <p>
        Rectification is an astrological technique for narrowing a probable birth time by working
        backward from known life events and testing candidate times against astrological timing
        techniques.
      </p>
      <p>
        It can produce a reasoned hypothesis; it does not recover a missing timestamp or verify a
        birth time in the way an external record can. Different practitioners may use different
        methods or reach different conclusions from the same life history, so the result should be
        described with the level of confidence the method supports.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a fuller record may still exist, it is worth checking that first. If you do pursue
        rectification, the dated life events in the next section are the kind of information a
        rectifier is likely to ask for.
      </p>

      <hr class="rule-print" />

      <h2 id="preparing">8 · Preparing for your reading</h2>
      <p>
        Use this summary to show the time you have, where it came from, and how confident you are in
        it. That gives the astrologer a clearer basis for deciding which parts of the chart can
        carry more interpretive weight.
      </p>
      <div class="ws">
        <div class="ws-grid">
          <div><span class="ws-label">Birth date</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">Birth city and country</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">Time (or range)</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
          <div><span class="ws-label">Confidence label</span><span class="ws-line"></span></div>
        </div>
        <span class="ws-label">What I am certain about</span>
        <span class="ws-line ws-line--tall"></span>
        <span class="ws-label">What I am unsure about</span>
        <span class="ws-line ws-line--tall"></span>
        <span class="ws-label">Dated life events that stand out: moves, losses, beginnings, turns</span>
        <span class="ws-line ws-line--tall"></span>
        <span class="ws-line ws-line--tall"></span>
        <span class="ws-label">The real question I want to bring</span>
        <span class="ws-line ws-line--tall"></span>
        <span class="ws-line ws-line--tall"></span>
      </div>

      <hr class="rule-print" />

      <p class="toolkit__close">
        That's the whole process. If you found the time, bring it. If you found a range, bring the
        range. If you found nothing, bring that. I would rather start from the information you
        actually have than from a tidy number carrying more certainty than it deserves.
      </p>
      <p class="no-print">
        <a class="btn btn--accent" href="/reading-finder/">Find the right reading</a>
        <a href="/birth-time/" style="margin-left: var(--space-4);">Back to the guide</a>
      </p>
      <p class="print-only">
        {site.name} · {site.url.replace('https://', '')} · Readings by Zoom or phone, with in-person
        appointments in the Dallas–Fort Worth area by arrangement.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style is:global media="screen">
  /* Browser-only worksheet presentation. The downloadable PDF is intentionally
     owned by scripts/gen-toolkit-pdf.mjs so screen styling cannot change its
     pagination. */
  .ws {
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
    padding: var(--space-5) var(--space-6);
    background: var(--bg-alt);
    border: var(--border-hairline);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }
  .ws-block + .ws-block {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    padding-top: var(--space-6);
    border-top: var(--border-hairline);
  }
  .ws-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3) var(--space-6);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }
  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .ws-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }
    .ws-grid--wide {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }
  }
  .ws-label {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-bottom: 0.15rem;
  }
  .ws-line {
    display: block;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-blue-muted);
    height: 1.6rem;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }
  .ws-line--tall {
    height: 2rem;
  }
  .ws-note {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
  }
  .ws-check {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-5);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }
  .ws-check li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: calc(1rem + var(--space-3));
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
  .ws-check li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.28rem;
    width: 1rem;
    height: 1rem;
    border: 1.5px solid var(--color-blue-muted);
    border-radius: 2px;
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }
  .ws-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
  .ws-table th {
    text-align: left;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    padding: 0 var(--space-2) var(--space-2) 0;
    border-bottom: 1.5px solid var(--color-blue-muted);
  }
  .ws-table td {
    height: 2.2rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-blue-muted);
    padding: 0 var(--space-2) 0 0;
  }
  .ws-scale {
    margin: var(--space-5) 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
  }
  .ws-scale dt {
    font-weight: 650;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .ws-scale dd {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .rule-print {
    border: none;
    border-top: var(--border-hairline);
    margin: var(--space-12) 0;
  }
  .print-only {
    display: none;
  }
</style>
